1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:05,520
Welcome to Video 2. Last time, you
turned your project into a story.

2
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:08,510
Now, let’s make that story believable.

3
00:00:08,510 --> 00:00:11,440
Every audience is quietly asking one question.

4
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:16,160
Will this actually work? On the left,
a claim with nothing behind it.

5
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:18,880
“It works really well, trust us.”

6
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:22,160
On the right, the same claim but backed up.

7
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:23,150
You tested it.

8
00:00:23,150 --> 00:00:27,960
You have one number with a source and
you’re honest about what’s still planned.

9
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:30,400
You need two tools to answer that question.

10
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:35,280
The first is a testing beat. The
second is one number that holds.

11
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:38,960
Together, they take about
20 seconds of your demo.

12
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:41,180
The testing beat has three parts.

13
00:00:41,180 --> 00:00:44,320
Let’s use our made-up project, Borrow Box.

14
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:50,240
First, what you assumed. The team assumed
new students wanted cheap basics to buy.

15
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:55,120
That’s a guess. So, before you
build more, ask three people.

16
00:00:55,120 --> 00:01:00,720
Second, what testers told you. One said,
“I only need a pot for a few weeks.”

17
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:04,080
Another said, “I don’t
know what I’ll keep yet.”

18
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:08,320
A third said, “Buying
everything costs too much.”

19
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:10,290
Third, what you changed.

20
00:01:10,290 --> 00:01:14,870
Borrow Box stopped selling and started
lending, with a date to bring things back.

21
00:01:14,870 --> 00:01:18,640
That change is your proof that you listened.

22
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:23,360
So, don’t say, “We did lots of
research and users loved it.”

23
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:27,280
Nobody can check that. Say
it in three sentences.

24
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:31,520
We assumed this, testers told
us that, so we changed it.

25
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:37,200
It takes about 15 seconds and it does more
for your credibility than any feature.

26
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:38,160
Now, numbers.

27
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:41,520
It’s tempting to fill a slide with figures,

28
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:46,480
but six numbers with no sources
give people nothing to remember.

29
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:50,720
Use one number and show where it comes from.

30
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:51,810
Why only one?

31
00:01:51,810 --> 00:01:54,080
In a 2007 study,

32
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:59,670
people who read about one child gave more
than twice as much as people who read

33
00:01:59,670 --> 00:02:04,960
statistics. And when the story came
with the statistics, giving went down.

34
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:08,000
Numbers can drown out the person.

35
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,200
So, handle your number in three steps.

36
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:14,880
State it in plain words,
show the source on screen,

37
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:18,020
then say what it proves and what it doesn’t.

38
00:02:18,020 --> 00:02:20,830
“It doesn’t prove our app fixes that.

39
00:02:20,830 --> 00:02:25,180
That’s what we’re testing.”
And put the person first.

40
00:02:25,180 --> 00:02:28,000
Introduce Amara, then give the number.

41
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,640
That way, the number explains her
story instead of floating on its own.

42
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:35,040
Finally, be honest about where you are.

43
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:40,560
Skip the big claims.
Instead, label things early.

44
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:45,440
Amara is an example persona.
Borrowing works today.

45
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:50,160
Return reminders come next. And
the listings are demo data.

46
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:55,520
A paper airplane is credible when it
doesn’t pretend to be a rocket ship.

47
00:02:55,520 --> 00:03:00,400
Your turn. One, show your
prototype to three people.

48
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:03,920
Two, write your testing
beat in three sentences.

49
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:07,120
Three, choose one number and find its source.

50
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:13,120
And four, finish the line.
“This shows ___, but not ___.”

51
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:19,120
Worksheet 3 in the handbook
has space for each of these.
